            <ArticleTitle>Crural and axial myoclonic dystonia following meralgia paraesthetica.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>176-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 49-year-old man developed a syndrome of crural-axial dystonia combined with segmental myoclonus 3 months after the onset of meralgia paraesthetica of the left leg. The association of this remarkable movement disorder with the pain syndrome is discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
